Refresh the packaged reference data for {{ source_name }}: $ARGUMENTS

Reference data the upstream publishes for every caller alike (code lists, attribute
lists, report calendars) may ship inside the package under
`src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/reference/` and is loaded with `xippios.core.reference`. It
comes from the upstream, never from memory. Not every source packages lists: one that
changes often stays a lookup task that calls upstream. A multi-tenant source
(sdk-core ADR-0036) packages no tenant data, only reference lists that are the same for
every tenant, if any. The change meets sdk-core's definition of done,
https://github.com/xippios/sdk-core/blob/main/docs/definition-of-done.md, and its review
checklist, https://github.com/xippios/sdk-core/blob/main/docs/review-checklist.md.

1. Identify the upstream list endpoint for each list named in the arguments, using
   `docs/upstream/`; a wrapper for it belongs in `api/` (`/add-endpoint`).
2. Declare the list in `LISTS` of `scripts/refresh_reference.py`, one `Listed` of
   `xippios.core.refresh`, as the script's examples show for its shape (a list of values,
   a list of integer ids and names, or a table: conventions §3): its name, which is its
   file `reference/<name>.json` and what `--only` takes; its fetch through the wrapper in
   `api/`; `endpoint`, the upstream operation it comes from; its `Enumeration`, if any;
   and its `origin` where its provenance row names more than that operation. Add its name
   to `PACKAGED_LISTS` in `params.py`, the names the script hands core's frame as
   `packaged`, which must be those of `LISTS` or the run stops before a request. Then run
   the script from the repository root with whatever the list endpoints need in the
   environment (a key only where they take one). Core's frame (sdk-core ADR-0051) fetches
   every list before it writes one, sending each request once with no retry, then writes
   each `reference/<name>.json` with `PackagedList.write` (the operation, the UTC time of
   the fetch, upstream's records), the list's row of the provenance table in
   `reference/README.md` and `enums.py`, one `render_enums` call formatted with
   `ruff format` and `ruff check --fix` (sdk-core ADR-0031), as one, so a run that fails
   writes nothing and is run again; commit what it leaves. After the fetches it prints the
   requests it sent, one that ended in a transport error among them, and the quota left
   that upstream reported, a failed run's too: say both in the pull request.
   `--only <list> ...` fetches the named lists alone and keeps every other list's file,
   so a change that packages a new list refreshes that list and no other.
   `uv run python scripts/refresh_reference.py --render-only` writes `enums.py` and the
   provenance rows from the packaged files alone, without a request or a key, as an
   adoption does after a core release changes the renderer (conventions §15, item 6).
   What a script writes beside the lists, an index or a manifest, goes in the hooks
   `before` and `after` of `run`, as the frame's docstring says.
3. Read the list's row in the provenance table of `reference/README.md`, which the run
   wrote: the file, its origin (the endpoint in a code span, or the `origin` its `Listed`
   declares, the URL it was fetched from or the `docs/upstream/` file it was rebuilt
   from) and the UTC time of the fetch (for a list rebuilt from `docs/upstream/`, its
   fetch builds the `PackagedList` with the snapshot's `Fetched` time). The run keeps the
   row of a file that no `Listed` declares as it stands.
4. Declare the list for the nightly live comparison in `tests/test_live.py`, which
   collects core's test (sdk-core ADR-0055): in `LIVE_LISTS`, by its name, its codes as
   the package declares them, `PackagedList.codes(...)`, and the wrapper in `api/` that
   fetches it now, or in `NO_LIVE_LIST` with the reason it is not compared, a list that
   no endpoint serves, say. Core's family check fails a packaged list that stands in
   neither, and an entry that names no packaged list.
5. If agents have no lookup task for this list yet, add one with `/add-endpoint` so they
   can look the codes up: one lookup task per list (`list_<thing>`), or the upstream's
   own lookup operation when it serves every list through one. A lookup over a packaged
   list returns the packaged data, it does not call upstream. A list an agent needs whole
   that is longer than the default result cap (200 rows or 64 KB) gets a cap of its own,
   `MCP_CAPS` in `tasks.py`, keyed by the task's name (sdk-core ADR-0038).
6. Update what pins the old lists: counts and dates in list-task docstrings and in
   tests. A test that reads the counts and the fetch date, `fetched`, from the packaged
   file needs no change (`/add-endpoint`, step 6), where a pinned `fetched` changes with
   every refresh. A test that compares a packaged list with the recorded answer of its
   list endpoint fails once a record changes: record those fixtures again in the same
   change with `/record-fixture`, and add their calls to the requests the refresh sent.
7. A changelog fragment under `changelog/`, as `changelog/README.md` lays it out, says
   which lists changed.
8. Run the checks,
   `uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check . && uv run mypy && uv run pytest && uv run pre-commit run --all-files`,
   and summarise the diff in the pull request: how many rows changed, any codes that
   disappeared (those may break existing fixtures or callers).
